  <section>
    <h2 id="hong-kong">Planning a Shenzhen + Hong Kong Itinerary</h2>
    <p>Hong Kong counts as a separate region for the transit test, as explained in the <a href="https://zw.china-embassy.gov.cn/tzzn/202508/P020250813008018401158.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chinese embassy’s entry guide (PDF)</a>. Its older nationality and port counts have since been superseded by NIA’s current policy.</p>
    <p>A <strong>direct U.S. → Shenzhen → Hong Kong</strong> trip may pass the route test. But flying from the U.S. via Hong Kong into Shenzhen, then returning to Hong Kong, does not: Hong Kong is immediately before and after Mainland China. Examine every connection, including the inbound flight’s last overseas stop.</p>
    <p>Even a route that passes still needs an eligible nationality, passport, entry port, confirmed onward ticket, permitted travel area and correct timing. A later flight home from Hong Kong does not replace proof of the journey that actually takes you out of Mainland China. Confirm your planned train, ferry or other onward evidence with immigration before relying on it.</p>

    <h3>Which Shenzhen and Guangdong Ports Can You Use?</h3>
    <p>NIA’s <a href="https://en.nia.gov.cn/n147418/n147463/c183412/part/194194.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">current port and stay-area table</a> lists these ten Guangdong entry ports:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Shenzhen Bao’an International Airport and Shekou Port (Passenger).</li>
      <li>Guangzhou Baiyun International Airport, Nansha Port (Passenger) and Guangzhou Pazhou Ferry Terminal.</li>
      <li>Jieyang Chaoshan International Airport and Zhongshan Port (Passenger).</li>
      <li>Hengqin Port, Hong Kong–Zhuhai–Macao Bridge Port and West Kowloon Station Port of the Guangzhou–Shenzhen–Hong Kong Express Rail Link.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>All of Guangdong, including Shenzhen, is a permitted stay area.</strong> The table explicitly allows exit at all open ports across the province. That does not make every crossing an eligible entry port: Luohu, Futian, Huanggang and Shenzhen Bay are not on this entry list. Check the exact crossing and its arrangements with NIA’s 12367 service before booking.</p>
    <p>West Kowloon’s Mainland immigration checkpoint is physically in Hong Kong. It is an eligible entry port following the <a href="https://en.nia.gov.cn/n147413/c188188/content.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">November 2025 expansion</a>. See the <a href="https://ka.sz.gov.cn/bmfw/en/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Shenzhen port authority’s checkpoint guide</a>. Hong Kong admission requirements apply separately, and leaving Mainland China ends that admission; any return requires a fresh qualifying entry basis.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="stay-length">How Long Can You Stay?</h2>
    <p>Under 240-hour transit, the authorized period is up to ten days. NIA calculates it from <strong>00:00 on the day after entry</strong>. Follow the expiry recorded on your temporary entry permission and schedule onward travel comfortably before it.</p>
    <p>You can plan arrival, all three fair days and departure within that period. Side trips must stay within the designated permitted areas; the policy does not authorize unrestricted travel across China. If you enter on a visa or another exemption, follow that permission’s stay limit instead.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="registration">How to Register as a Visitor</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Start from the <a href="https://www.chtf.com/english/Info/ForVisitors/Notice/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official visitor badge page</a> and follow its “Register Now” link. Alternatively, use the official CHTF WeChat account.</li>
      <li>Complete registration confirmation and the questionnaire with your real identity details and a regularly used mobile number. Use details matching your passport.</li>
      <li>Check approval and save the electronic admission confirmation/QR code sent to your email or phone.</li>
      <li>Bring the passport used for registration and your saved admission code. Ask the organizer for help if the system cannot accept your passport or overseas phone number.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The <a href="https://www.chtf.com/english/Info/ForVisitors/First/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">English first-time visitor guide</a> describes repeated entry during the three-day event for approved visitors. Overseas visitor enquiries: <a href="mailto:sarah@zhenweiexpo.com">sarah@zhenweiexpo.com</a>.</p>

    <h3>Tickets and the Free Preregistration Deadline</h3>
    <p>The <a href="https://www.chtf.com/canguanzhiyin/gonggao/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2026 ticket notice (Chinese)</a> says registration completed <strong>before 12:00 noon on November 25</strong> is free; late registration costs <strong>RMB 120</strong>. Electronic tickets can be used by the registered person for multiple visits across the exhibition dates. Ticket sales/registration end at 15:00 on November 28, ahead of the 16:00 last-entry time.</p>
    <p>Register well before the deadline to allow time for approval or corrections. The English pages do not repeat the fee/deadline detail; confirm any passport-specific payment or registration issue with the organizer. Chrome can translate the Chinese notice into English: right-click the page and choose “Translate to English.”</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="admission">Foreign Visitor Admission Requirements</h2>
    <p>Bring your <strong>original passport, electronic badge/QR code and registration confirmation</strong>. The badge notice describes passport scanning, while the <a href="https://www.chtf.com/english/Info/ForVisitors/Admission/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">English admission page</a> describes QR-code entry. Carry both and allow time for staff assistance.</p>
    <p>The English admission page directs unregistered overseas visitors to the on-site Overseas Visitors window. The <a href="https://www.chtf.com/canguanzhiyin/ruchangxuzhiX/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chinese admission instructions (Chinese)</a> direct foreign visitors to visitor services. On-site assistance is not a promise of free entry or a substitute for advance registration.</p>
    <h3>Can Minors Attend?</h3>
    <p><strong>The official rules conflict.</strong> The Chinese admission instructions exclude visitors under 16, and the English page says visitors must be above 16. But the Chinese ticket notice allows under-16s free entry without advance booking when accompanied by a guardian who signs an on-site minors’ waiver; it says those aged 16 and above register normally.</p>
    <p>Ask the organizer to confirm admission for anyone under 16 or exactly 16 before making travel plans. Both admission pages say visitors over 70 must be accompanied by an adult and hold a valid ticket. Expect security checks at the entrance. Chrome can translate the Chinese instructions into English.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="invitation-letters">Invitation Letters for a China Visa</h2>
    <p>The <a href="https://www.chtf.com/english/Info/ForVisitors/visaInformation/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official CHTF visa information page</a> provides an invitation template. Download it, fill it in and send it to <a href="mailto:sarah@zhenweiexpo.com">sarah@zhenweiexpo.com</a> for an official signature and stamp.</p>
    <p>The page does not publish a turnaround time or guarantee approval. Allow time to confirm the process and the requirements of the visa office handling your application. CHTF states that the letter does not guarantee a visa and that attendees are responsible for obtaining their entry paperwork.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="us-visitors">Attending from the United States</h2>
    <p>U.S. ordinary-passport holders are on NIA’s eligible transit list. A qualifying onward trip may therefore work for CHTF, but an ordinary U.S. → Shenzhen → U.S. return trip fails the transit-route test. Arrange an appropriate visa unless another applicable exemption covers you.</p>
    <p>Check connections carefully when comparing fares via Hong Kong. See our <a href="/do-us-citizens-need-a-visa-for-china/">Do U.S. Citizens Need a Visa for China?</a> guide for U.S.-specific planning.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="transport">Venue, Airports and Ground Transportation</h2>
    <p>CHTF 2026 is at <strong>Shenzhen World Exhibition &amp; Convention Center in Bao’an</strong>, at No. 1 Zhancheng Road, Fuhai Street. Save the Chinese address for a driver: <span lang="zh-CN">深圳市宝安区福海街道和平社区展城路1号</span>.</p>
    <p>Use the complete venue name when searching maps or hotels. Do not confuse it with the Shenzhen Convention &amp; Exhibition Center in Futian.</p>
    <h3>Shenzhen Bao’an Airport vs Other Arrival Options</h3>
    <p><strong>Shenzhen Bao’an International Airport (SZX)</strong> is the most convenient airport for venue proximity. Shenzhen World’s <a href="https://www.shenzhen-world.com/overview/traffic/en/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official transport guide</a> places the venue about 7 km from Terminal 3; this is not a guaranteed road journey time.</p>
    <p>Hong Kong International Airport can be practical when flights suit your schedule, but adds a border crossing and onward ground or ferry arrangements. Choose your Mainland entry port before choosing the transfer. Guangzhou Baiyun is another eligible airport, with a longer onward journey to Shenzhen. Compare the entire door-to-door itinerary, including immigration, luggage and transfers.</p>
    <h3>Metro Access to Shenzhen World</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Lines 12 and 20 — Shenzhen World (国展):</strong> exits C1/C2 serve the South Lobby.</li>
      <li><strong>Lines 12 and 20 — Shenzhen World North (国展北):</strong> exits C1/C2 serve the North Lobby.</li>
      <li><strong>From Airport (T3):</strong> take Line 11 to Airport North, then transfer to Line 20 toward the venue.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The <a href="https://www.sz.gov.cn/en_szgov/life/transport/metro/content/post_12870342.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Shenzhen government Metro guide</a> lists these lines and airport stations. Choose the lobby nearest your exhibition hall and recheck current services before travel.</p>
    <p>Taxis or prearranged car transfers can simplify arrival with luggage. Allow extra time for fair traffic and security. Confirm 2026 shuttle routes, pickup points and any transport benefits with CHTF; do not assume a previous edition’s services will operate.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="hotels">Where to Stay</h2>
    <h3>Near Shenzhen World or the airport</h3>
    <p>For three days focused on the fair, prioritize a hotel in the Bao’an exhibition area with a clear route to your lobby. Check the actual entrance-to-entrance journey: “near the exhibition center” can refer to a different venue or a large surrounding district.</p>
    <h3>In Nanshan or Futian</h3>
    <p>These areas may suit other meetings or sightseeing, but involve a longer commute. Compare Metro connections and the morning journey before booking. Staying in Hong Kong would add border formalities on each fair day; assess each Mainland entry separately if relying on transit.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="checklist">Travel Checklist</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Confirm event dates, hours, hall and admission rules on the official site.</li>
      <li>Identify your Mainland entry basis and, for transit, test the immediately preceding/following countries or regions.</li>
      <li>Verify passport validity, entry port, onward reservation, permitted destinations and authorized departure deadline.</li>
      <li>Complete preregistration early and save approval, QR code and organizer contact details.</li>
      <li>Carry your original passport and any required visa or invitation documents.</li>
      <li>Save your hotel booking, Chinese venue address and airport-to-hotel route offline.</li>
      <li>Confirm Hong Kong or other onward-destination entry requirements and allow a buffer for border checks.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details><summary>Does a CHTF badge let me enter China without a visa?</summary><p>No. Fair admission and immigration permission are separate. Your passport and itinerary must qualify for a visa exemption, or you need the appropriate visa.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Can I attend all three days under 240-hour transit?</summary><p>Potentially, if your complete trip meets the transit requirements and you depart within your authorized stay. Check your admission confirmation separately for fair access.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Does a trip via Hong Kong automatically qualify?</summary><p>No. Hong Kong → Shenzhen → Hong Kong fails the route test even if your wider trip starts in another country. A different preceding and following country or region is only one of the eligibility conditions.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Is visitor registration free?</summary><p>The Chinese ticket notice lists free preregistration before noon on November 25, 2026 and a RMB 120 fee afterward. Recheck the notice before registering.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Can I bring my child?</summary><p>CHTF’s ticket and admission pages give conflicting rules for minors. Confirm your child’s age and admission arrangements directly with the organizer before booking.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Which Metro station serves CHTF?</summary><p>Shenzhen World and Shenzhen World North on Lines 12 and 20 serve the South and North Lobbies respectively. Check which lobby is closest to your hall.</p></details>
  </section>

